---
description: Run borderlint before adding any AI SDK, AI endpoint, model identifier, or MCP server configuration, including stdio command/args — check residency, sovereignty, and provenance before committing.
alwaysApply: true
---

Before adding any AI SDK import, AI endpoint reference, model identifier, or structured MCP server
configuration to this codebase (an `openai`/`anthropic`/`langchain`-style package, a provider URL,
a Bedrock/HF/Ollama model id, an OpenAI-compatible `base_url`, an LLM endpoint in config or `.env`,
or an MCP server under `mcpServers` (VS Code: `servers`) in `.mcp.json`,
`claude_desktop_config.json`, `.cursor/mcp.json`, or `.vscode/mcp.json`, using a remote `url` or
stdio `command` and `args`):

1. Run the border check first. If the project has a policy, discover its path and substitute it
   for `<project-policy-path>`:
   `borderlint scan . --policy <project-policy-path> --classification <the project's data class>`
   Otherwise inventory mode: `borderlint scan .`
2. If the addition would introduce a flow whose jurisdiction is not `local` — or a
   provider/router that resolves to `unknown` — say so in the conversation BEFORE committing,
   naming the provider, its residency jurisdiction, its sovereignty bloc, and (if a model id is
   involved) its provenance bloc. Let the human decide.
3. If the flow is accepted, record it with an inline waiver using the file's comment syntax and a
   required, real justification: `# borderlint: allow <justification>` or
   `// borderlint: allow <justification>`. Unjustified waivers are ignored; waivers cannot
   override a provider or model deny.

Backstop: the pre-commit hook enforces its configured scan. The CI SBOM `diff` gate fails when
the current SBOM adds a non-`local` provider/jurisdiction pair (including `unknown`) absent from
the baseline, not for every new endpoint or model. This rule moves the check to where the decision
is made.

Install: save as `.cursor/rules/borderlint.mdc` in your project.
